  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CASE-Community for forest management</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Promoting community to Participation in Village Forest Management Project in Xiengkhouang Province, which anticipates the following results:
Changes in person:
The group of ethnic women who participated in the project will gained more knowledge andskill aboutvillage forest management and were courageous, outspoken, and community leaders, ethnic women will have the self-confidence to leadethnic women groups among village and project activities and to mobilize women to participate in project activities, as well as to write action plans, reports and community activities.
The CASE staffs will be able to encourage ethnic women in activities includingcoordination with partners, planning, implementation, reporting, monitoring and evaluation.
At the internal of CASE:
CASE Encouragemulti-stakeholder to participation in community development. Therefore, the implementation of the project will enable the CASE to have ethnic staff with the knowledge and ability to represent the organization in participating in projects at both the local and central levels. In addition to the organization, there will be leaders who carry out ethnic activities and aim to share lessons with other organizations
Ethnic Group:
Indigenous women have improved, for example, ascommunity leaders, facilitators, project implementers, and able to disseminate knowledge and understanding to ethnic women through peer-to-peer
External change:
Ethnic women are courageous and involved in protecting the village forest and enriching theenvironment. At the same time, there is a process of sharing lessons of the community of learning and making leaders in the community.
In addition, forest areas are increasing as a result of forestconservation, including forest resources as a source offood and nutrition.
We will not decide or implement to carry out any activities, programs or policies without the direct involvement of the members of the stakeholder group, they will be involved all term of the project period and being the ownership with all activity related. Therefore, the implementation of a project, activity or policyrequires to let the participation understand and welling to implement as their own asset of those affected by the activities. Therefore, CASE considers ethnic women groupsas important key factor in identifying activities and planning, as well as conducting activities so that they can be involved in the process from the planning, implementation and mutual benefit.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CASE is the non-profit organisation in Laos, which established since 2015 with their vision is to Better living condition of community on the poverty reduction with green approach and sustainable. Also the main goals of organisation is to 1. The food, nutrition and income generation aim to encourage villagers to have nutrition food at all seasons and earn more income;
2. Natural resources: Topromote management, protection and sustainable use of natural resources.
3. Environment: In order to encourage the use less of chemicals, waste has an environmental impact
4. Organizational Development: To enhance the capacity of APEDC to implement effectiveness and efficiency to fulfill its mission.These vision and goals are to help those rightsholder which including with
Youth, Children, women, Disadvantaged people, Disabled with people in Laos.
on the other hand CASE is former APEDC APECD is one of Civil Society Organization (CSO), locate in Xeingkhuang (XK) province, Lao PDR and established since 2015 basedon provincial governor agreement letter issued no. 478/XK. The goal of APECD is to provide the social services especially preservation of natural resources, environment, andcommunity development within Xiengkhuang province with total 23 members including5 board members, 5 staff member, and 2 volunteers.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Community Association for Salvation</narrative>
